    title: 'When the Tongue Holds the Keys of Death and Life',
    titleAr: 'حين يمسك اللسان مفاتيح الموت والحياة',
    image: img('1475721027785-f74eccf877e2'),
    tags: ['words', 'the heart', 'Christ the Word', 'speech'],
    tagsAr: ['الكلمات', 'القلب', 'المسيح الكلمة', 'اللسان'],
    excerpt: '"Death and life are in the power of the tongue." One word can kill a person\'s dignity, and one word can revive a hope that had died. Guarding the tongue starts in the heart.',
    excerptAr: '«اَلْمَوْتُ وَالْحَيَاةُ فِي يَدِ اللِّسَانِ». كلمة واحدة قد تقتل كرامة إنسان، وكلمة واحدة قد توقظ رجاءً مات.',
    content: `
"Death and life are in the power of the tongue."
What a frightening truth! Scripture places in the "hand" of a small member, with no bone and no weapon, a power that may exceed the sword: to kill or to give life.
A single word can kill a person's dignity, destroy a marriage, plant despair in a child's heart, or push a burdened soul into deeper brokenness. On the other hand, a single word can awaken a hope that had died, restore a person's sense of worth, and open a window in a wall of darkness.
Even more serious, Solomon does not say that death and life are "around" the tongue, but that they are "in its hand." We do not simply speak and the story ends. Our words become seeds, and then we come back to eat their fruit: "and those who love it will eat its fruit" (Proverbs 18:21).
But here the human tragedy is exposed. We have a tongue that wants to give life, while the heart that moves it carries within it the seeds of death. That is why Christ says, "Out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks" (Matthew 12:34). The problem is deeper than refining our words; it is a heart that needs to be changed.
So it is not enough to learn how to say nicer words. We need a new life that makes the source of our words new.
Here the Gospel takes us to a great mystery: "In the beginning was the Word… and the Word was God… In Him was life" (John 1:1, 4).
What a holy paradox! With our tongues we can speak words that kill, but God sent the Word in whom is life. We bring out of our mouths words that cannot create anything, but Christ, the living Word of God, came into a world of death to give life.
At the cross this truth reaches its peak: the One through whom life existed before death entered death Himself to bring us out into life.
Christ did not come only to teach us how to speak, but to change the heart that speaks.
So guarding the tongue begins deeper than the lips: in the heart that needs Christ.
The question is not only, "What did I say today?" but, "Which heart spoke through me?"
Rev. Radi Atallah`,
